ake the promise that one of the two of  them is gonna be the world's best  swordsman one day the next morning after  their fight and after the big promise  sari comes into the dojo just to find  out that Queen actually had fallen down  the stairs while trying to get a  sharpening block and died and sorrow was  a really really great pain at that and  was really angry with her and accusing  her of running away from the promise  they made the previous night before and  finally by overcoming a little bit of  his pain Sarah asks Queen s father for  the honor of taking the Votto Jumanji  the sword and Queen aboard to take it  with him and he gets the permission and  with this sword Sora promises to fulfill  the dream the two of them had in her  stead as well and to become the greatest  swordsman for the sake of both of them  and what's interesting about that is  that there are a lot of theories that  queen is still alive and we'll see her  again wha know or that she's actually  the comet of smoker which actually has  been debunked since Oda has actually  drawn sushi Kia's as a child which  actually doesn't look like Queen at all  and in my opinion there would be no  point in Queen uh still being alive  since the point of her death being that  it was just so frustratingly meaningless  and in my opinion that is what really  makes out great character building  because it was a huge lesson that Sarah  had to learn that someone could actually  die or someone as honorable and someone  as skilled as Queen I could die not from  fighting as another strong swordsman or  being defeated by him or or someone else  in an honorable battle but actually die  by a stupid thing like falling down the  stairs and that is actually the tragic  thing Sora learns in that moment and in  my opinion is a great part of the  character building for him so I think  the way that Oda handled this is on the  one hand side frustratingly cruel and  frustratingly unnecessary but at the  same time also a very 